## 12.5: every encode asserted, two shapes, sequential and concurrent
#  `shared` is one facade object handed to every thread, which is the analogue
#  of the shared client the rust slice tripped over. `per-thread` is one each.
#
#  The reference is THE ARM'S OWN output, taken once before any thread starts,
#  and `canon` says whether that equals the manifest's canonical bytes. It is
#  not the manifest itself, because `SerializeToString` does not sort map
#  entries and the canonical form does, so the incumbent legally writes another
#  accepted form on M2 -- which conformance.py checks and this suite must not
#  re-litigate. The question here is whether CONCURRENCY changes the bytes.
   shape  arm                      canon objects     threads  encodes    wrong
   P1.2   upb (incumbent)          yes   per-thread  seq          120        0
   P1.2   upb (incumbent)          yes   per-thread  2             80        0
